{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>You shall not go about as a talebearer among your people</b> — I say that because all who send strife and recount evil speech go about in the houses of their fellows to spy out what evil they may see or what evil they may hear, to tell it in the market, they are called הוֹלְכֵי רָכִיל (goers of talebearing), הוֹלְכֵי רְגִילָה (goers of spying), and so on. And so Onkelos translated (Leviticus 19:16): לָא תֵיכוֹל קֻרְצִין, (Daniel 3:8): וַאֲכַלוּ קַרְצֵיהוֹן דִּי יְהוּדָיֵא, (Bava Metzia 86a): אֲכַלוּ קוּרְצָא בֵּי מַלְכָּא. It appears to me that their practice was to eat at the house of the one who receives their words some kind of feeding (הַלְעָטָה), and it is a conclusion of reinforcement, that his words are upheld and he establishes them as truth. And that feeding is called אֲכִילַת קוּרְצִין, in the language of (Proverbs 6:13): קוֹרֵץ בְּעֵינָיו (winks with his eyes), for so is the way of the talebearer to wink with his eyes and to hint at words of talebearing so that the listeners will not understand — all this is the language of the Rav.\n\nBut there is no sense or reason in what he explained in this translation, for the one who hears from the talebearer does not swear to him that he will believe his words, nor does he give him a sign or a proof, and also when one slanders a servant to his master, the master does not assure him that he will listen to him — so what is the reason for this eating? And Nebuchadnezzar, in the testimony of the righteous ones, did what he did: he did not feed the slanderers, nor did he swear to them, nor did he believe them, but rather asked (Daniel 3:14): 'Is it true, Shadrach, Meshach…?', and commanded that they bow down from now on, and he would pass over what they had transgressed. And Darius did not feed Daniel's slanderers anything but grass and roots, and it is written of them (Daniel 6:25 [6:26 in many editions]): דִּי אֲכַלוּ קַרְצוֹהִי דִּי דָנִיֵּאל. And even if it were true that this was done in those times, after the verse says 'You shall not go about as a talebearer among your people,' why would Onkelos mention that foolish practice, which has nothing to do with the prohibition?\n\nRather, the root meaning of the Aramaic term here is nothing but the language of producing a sound, and it is common in the words of the Sages (Bava Batra 5a): וְעִזָּא לָאו אַכְלוֹיֵי מְכַלּוּ לֵיהּ וְלָאו גַּבְרָא בָּעֵי לְאַכְלוֹיֵי (the goats do not need to call him; the man does not need to call them), and Jonathan translated: (Isaiah 58:1) קְרָא בְגָרוֹן (cry with the throat) — אַכְלֵי; (Isaiah 5:26) וְשָׁרַק לוֹ מִקְצֵה הָאָרֶץ — וְיַכְלֵי לֵיהּ; (Isaiah 5:30) וְיִנְהֹם עָלָיו כְּנַהֲמַת יָם — וְיַכְלֵי עֲלֵיהוֹן; and similarly in many places. And so it is the language of every one who makes a sound — who announces his desire without cutting words short — and therefore they say thus (in that same passage) about one calling to the goats entering the field, and so they translate the one who whistles, the one who murmurs, and the one who cries out. And the way of the talebearers is to come in public or before the ruler, and they murmur with their throats and wink with their eyes to hint that they have heard things, until people press them and they tell them; therefore they are called אוֹכְלֵי קֻרְצִין — those who murmur in hints. And Onkelos translated רְכִילוּת according to its meaning, and so is its name in the Aramaic tongue, and he was not concerned to explain the language of the verse, and so is always his way, for to convey the meaning is his intent.\n\nBut in the sacred tongue they would call them הוֹלְכֵי רָכִיל, from (Song of Songs 3:6): אַבְקַת רוֹכֵל, (Ezekiel 26:12): רְכֻלָּתֵךְ, for the רוֹכֵל (peddler) goes about all day buying from here and from there, and goes and sells in other places here and there, as the Sages mention (Mishnah Ma'aserot 2:3): רוֹכְלִין הַמְּחַזְּרִין בָּעֲיָרוֹת (peddlers who go around the villages). And this is the reason for 'among your people' (בְּעַמֶּךָ), for he goes about in public. And to distinguish between the two, this name רוֹכֵל is a verb (פֹּעַל) and this name רָכִיל is a descriptive noun (שֵׁם תֹּאַר) in itself, like סָרִיס, נָזִיר — the name hints that it is inherent in his very person, and to him it refers:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The Rav referred to is Maimonides, who interprets אכילת קורצין literally as 'eating'; Ramban first presents this view, then disputes it and offers his own linguistic analysis. The phrase וְהוּא גְּמַר חִזּוּץ is difficult and rendered hyper-literally; it may mean a finalized act of strengthening/confirming."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:56:33.692235414Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}